{"id":"signin","corpus":"aws","title":"AWS Sign-In","sourceUrl":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/signin/latest/userguide/what-is-sign-in.html","license":"AWS Documentation © Amazon Web Services, Inc.","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"AWS Sign-In"},{"kind":"p","text":"We're currently releasing our new experience to a limited number of customers. You might not be able to access this experience yet."},{"kind":"p","text":"This guide helps you understand the different ways that you can sign in to Amazon Web Services (AWS). You can sign in to AWS in the following ways:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Sign in for AWS"},{"kind":"li","text":"Sign in to our new AWS experience – Sign in using a method you already have, like Google or GitHub, and access projects with preconfigured defaults."},{"kind":"li","text":"Sign up for AWS (advanced) – Sign in using an AWS access portal, a federated identity, or as a root user or IAM user."},{"kind":"li","text":"Sign in with AWS Builder ID – Sign in to your personal profile that provides access to select tools and services."},{"kind":"p","text":"For more information comparing our new sign up experience and the existing AWS experience, see Compare sign-up options."},{"kind":"p","text":"If you have issues signing in to AWS, see the troubleshooting documentation for each way to sign in:"},{"kind":"li","text":"If you're having trouble signing into your AWS account, see Troubleshooting AWS account sign-in issues."},{"kind":"li","text":"If you're having trouble signing into our new AWS experience, see Troubleshooting our new AWS experience issues."},{"kind":"li","text":"If you're having trouble signing into your AWS Builder ID to access select AWS tools or services, see Troubleshooting AWS Builder ID issues."},{"kind":"p","text":"For more information about how signing up for AWS can help you or your organization, see Contact Us."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Terminology"},{"kind":"p","text":"Amazon Web Services (AWS) uses common terminology to describe the sign in process. We recommend you read and understand these terms."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Administrator"},{"kind":"p","text":"Also referred to as an AWS account administrator or IAM administrator. The administrator, typically Information Technology (IT) personnel, is an individual who oversees an AWS account. Administrators have a higher level of permissions to the AWS account than other members of their organization. Administrators establish and implement settings for the AWS account. They also create IAM or IAM Identity Center users. The administrator provides these users with their access credentials and a sign-in URL to sign in to AWS."},{"kind":"p","text":"An administrator is only supported if you sign in with Sign up for AWS (advanced)."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Account"},{"kind":"p","text":"A standard AWS account contains both your AWS resources and the identities that can access those resources. Accounts are associated with the account owner’s email address and password."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Credentials"},{"kind":"p","text":"Also referred to as access credentials or security credentials. In authentication and authorization, a system uses credentials to identify who is making a call and whether to allow the requested access. Credentials are the information that users provide to AWS to sign in and gain access to AWS resources. Credentials for human users can include an email address, a user name, a user defined password, an account ID or alias, a verification code, and a single use multi-factor authentication (MFA) code. For programmatic access, you can also use access keys. We recommend using short-term access keys when possible."},{"kind":"p","text":"For more information about credentials, see AWS security credentials."},{"kind":"p","text":"The type of credentials a user must submit depends on their user type."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Corporate credentials"},{"kind":"p","text":"The credentials that users provide when accessing their corporate network and resources. Your corporate administrator can set up your AWS account to use the same credentials that you use to access your corporate network and resources. These credentials are provided to you by your administrator or help desk employee."},{"kind":"p","text":"Corporate credentials are only supported if you sign in with Sign up for AWS (advanced)."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Profile"},{"kind":"p","text":"When you sign up for an AWS Builder ID or use our new AWS experience, you create a profile. Your profile includes the contact information you provided and the ability to manage multi-factor authentication (MFA) devices and active sessions. You can also learn more about privacy and how we handle your data in your profile. For more information about how your Builder ID relates to an AWS account, see AWS Builder ID and other AWS credentials."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Root user credentials"},{"kind":"p","text":"The root user credentials are the email address and password used to create the AWS account. We strongly recommend that MFA be added to the root user credentials for additional security. Root user credentials provide complete access to all AWS services and resources in the account. For more information on the root user, see Root user."}],"lemmas":{"signs":["sign","signs"],"signed":["signed","sign"],"we'res":["we'res","we're"],"currentlies":["currentlies","currently"],"releasing":["releasing","release"],"releasings":["releasings","releasing"],"releases":["release","releases"],"released":["released","release"],"experiences":["experience","experiences"],"experienced":["experienced","experience"],"experiencing":["experiencing","experience"],"limited":["limited","limit"],"limiteds":["limited","limiteds"],"limits":["limit","limits"],"limiting":["limiting","limit"],"numbers":["number","numbers"],"numbering":["numbering","number"],"numbered":["numbered","number"],"customers":["customer","customers"],"nots":["nots","not"],"ables":["ables","able"],"accesses":["access","accesses"],"accessed":["a